From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Subject: [PATCH] Skip a sense period read after a probe closed the speakers

When an audio server probes the speaker PCM at startup (open, set the
hardware parameters, close), the sense PCM can deliver a period that
speakersafetyd reads only after the kernel has already reported the
speakers closed ("Speaker Sample Rate" back to 0). If no rate was ever
known since the sense PCM was opened, speakersafetyd panicked with
"Invalid sample rate" and systemd restarted it. On an M2 MacBook Air
that happened on every boot and on every PipeWire restart, and repeated
restarts can reach the unit's start limit, which leaves the speakers
muted at the kernel's floor.

Skip such a period: it cannot be modelled without a rate, and it belongs
to an open that has already closed. The skipped time is not credited as
cooling. Every other invalid rate (negative values) still panics, so the
daemon fails safe as before. A known rate is kept after the speakers
close, as before.

Downstream (Aurora Silicon) patch on speakersafetyd 3.0.0 (nixpkgs); the 2.0.1 form ships in fedora-aurora and alarm-aurora.
---
diff --git a/src/main.rs b/src/main.rs
index d4a3859..d0cfda7 100644
--- a/src/main.rs
+++ b/src/main.rs
@@ -48,6 +48,57 @@ struct Options {
     max_reduction: Option<f32>,
 }
 
+/// The rate to model one sense period with.
+#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
+enum PeriodRate {
+    /// Model the period at this rate.
+    Known(i32),
+    /// No rate was ever known since open and the speakers are closed again:
+    /// a probe open that closed before its period was read.
+    ProbeClosed,
+    /// Anything else is not a rate: fail safe (the kernel holds the floor).
+    Invalid,
+}
+
+/// `known` is the last rate seen since the PCM was opened (0: none yet),
+/// `cur` what "Speaker Sample Rate" reads now (0: speakers closed).
+fn period_rate(known: i32, cur: i32) -> PeriodRate {
+    if cur < 0 || known < 0 {
+        PeriodRate::Invalid
+    } else if cur > 0 {
+        PeriodRate::Known(cur)
+    } else if known > 0 {
+        PeriodRate::Known(known)
+    } else {
+        PeriodRate::ProbeClosed
+    }
+}
+
+#[cfg(test)]
+mod period_rate_tests {
+    use super::{period_rate, PeriodRate};
+
+    #[test]
+    fn a_probe_period_before_any_rate_is_skipped() {
+        assert_eq!(period_rate(0, 0), PeriodRate::ProbeClosed);
+    }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn a_known_rate_is_kept_after_the_speakers_close() {
+        assert_eq!(period_rate(48000, 0), PeriodRate::Known(48000));
+        assert_eq!(period_rate(48000, 48000), PeriodRate::Known(48000));
+        assert_eq!(period_rate(0, 48000), PeriodRate::Known(48000));
+        assert_eq!(period_rate(44100, 48000), PeriodRate::Known(48000));
+    }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn any_other_invalid_rate_still_fails() {
+        assert_eq!(period_rate(0, -1), PeriodRate::Invalid);
+        assert_eq!(period_rate(48000, -48000), PeriodRate::Invalid);
+        assert_eq!(period_rate(-1, 0), PeriodRate::Invalid);
+    }
+}
+
 fn get_machine() -> String {
     fs::read_to_string("/proc/device-tree/compatible")
         .expect("Could not read device tree compatible")
@@ -288,13 +339,25 @@ fn main() {
 
             let cur_sample_rate = sample_rate_elem.read_int(&ctl);
 
-            if cur_sample_rate != 0 && cur_sample_rate != sample_rate {
-                sample_rate = cur_sample_rate;
-                info!("Sample rate: {}", sample_rate);
-            }
-
-            if sample_rate == 0 {
-                panic!("Invalid sample rate");
+            match period_rate(sample_rate, cur_sample_rate) {
+                PeriodRate::Known(rate) => {
+                    if rate != sample_rate {
+                        sample_rate = rate;
+                        info!("Sample rate: {}", sample_rate);
+                    }
+                }
+                PeriodRate::ProbeClosed => {
+                    // No rate has been known since the PCM was opened, and the
+                    // kernel already reports the speakers closed: this period
+                    // came from a speaker open that closed before it was read
+                    // (an audio server probing the device at startup). It
+                    // cannot be modelled without a rate. Skip it, and do not
+                    // credit the skipped time as cooling.
+                    warn!("Sense period read after the speakers closed, before any rate was known; skipping it");
+                    last_update = Instant::now();
+                    continue;
+                }
+                PeriodRate::Invalid => panic!("Invalid sample rate"),
             }
 
             let now = Instant::now();
